UID:2e133fed7622b79d9a7e83b6842e93@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Newburyport Art (NAA) presents three new shows opening in the 
 month of May! Soft Structures is a group exhibition that explores fibers a
 nd fibrous material as process and metaphor. This exhibit features over 80
  artworks in a variety of media including drawing\, painting\, sculpture\,
  weaving\, embroidery\, quilting\, collage\, mixed media and more. This ex
 hibit is on view in the first-floor Sargent Gallery and second-floor Harts
 on Gallery. FOLIA is a collaborative exhibition featuring works by Amesbur
 y artists Amy Vander Els & Heather Karp. This exhibit explores the quiet b
 eauty and joyful rhythm of leaves and the symbolic meaning behind many tha
 t speaks of perseverance\, resilience and hope. Thirty-six artworks are di
 splayed amongst spring greenery in the first-floor Hills Gallery. Amy and 
 Heather will give a free artist talk on Thursday\, May 21st from 6:00-7:00
 pm\, concluding with Q&A. The 11th Annual Outdoor Sculpture Garden Exhibit
 ion debuted on May 1st\, and will remain on view year-round until April 20
 27. At nearly 3\,000 square feet\, the Range Lights Sculpture Garden resid
 es behind the historic NAA and is seen and visited by hundreds of people e
 very week. This year’s exhibition features 16 pieces by 10 local and reg
 ional artists. The participating artists include\; Jeffrey Briggs\, Lindle
 y Briggs\, Sarah Burns and Mike Platt\, Alexis Guieb-Alimbuyuguen\, Andrew
  Henschel\, Mae Kenny\, Evan Ralph\, Michael Updike and Raynor Warner. A r
 eception for FOLIA and Soft Structures will be held on Saturday\, May 2nd 
 from 4-6pm\, with the 11th Annual Sculpture Exhibit reception beginning at
  3:00pm\, including a Best in Show award at 3:30pm. The receptions are fre
 e and open to the public\, and visitors participating in the May 2nd Newbu
 ryport Artwalk are encouraged to stop by. New Hours: Wednesday through Sun
 day from 11:00am–5:00pm. The gallery is handicap accessible. More inform
 ation\, as well as an online gallery is available at newburyportart.org. F
 ounded in 1948\, Newburyport Art\, a 501(c)(3) non-profit\, is dedicated t
 o promoting and exhibiting members’ work and to providing art education 
 and access to the visual arts for the entire community. The gallery and ev
 ents are free and open to the public.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent p
 age: https://northofboston.org/event/newburyport-art-presents-may-exhibiti
 ons-at-65-water-street/2026-12-05/
LOCATION:Newburyport Art Association\, 65 Water St\, Newburyport\, MA\, 01
 950
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/newburyport-art-presents-may-exhibitio
 ns-at-65-water-street/2026-12-05/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Once Upon a Winter: Tales of Wonder Through a Child’s Eyes
DTSTART:20261205T160000
DTEND:20261205T170000
DTSTAMP:20261115T180125Z
UID:fb45c4770fe12c6ae36571e91b81ef@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Cantemus Chamber Chorus\, with 42 members from across the Nort
 h Shore\, will present three concerts in December titled “Once Upon a Wi
 nter: Tales of Wonder Through a Child’s Eyes” in Newburyport\, Beverly
 \, and Gloucester. The concerts feature Matthew Harris’s “A Child’s 
 Christmas in Wales\,” based on the beloved holiday tale by Dylan Thomas.
  In addition\, the program will include a rich tapestry of contemporary an
 d traditional repertoire from different cultures reflecting themes of wond
 er\, home\, darkness\, returning light\, and memory. The repertoire includ
 es R. Murray Schafer’s “Snowforms\; “Song for Yalda Night\,” an Ir
 anian piece about the longest night of the year\; the “Kang Ding Love So
 ng\,” a Chinese folk song associated with the Winter Solstice\; Morten L
 auridsen’s “Lament for Pasiphaë\;” traditional Hanukkah songs “Mi
  Y’Maleil” and “S’vivon\;” Daniel Kantor’s “Night of Silence
 ” and more. In addition to accompanist Jeffrey Mead on piano\, joining C
 antemus will be an 11-piece chamber orchestra and the Lexington Children
 ’s Chorus (LCC)\, an auditioned choral ensemble for singers in grades 4-
 6. Founded by Artistic Director Allison Iannuzzi in 2019\, the LCC has col
 laborated with the Lexington Symphony Orchestra\, the Lexington High Schoo
 l Choral Program\, the Middlesex County Volunteer Fifes & Drums\, and othe
 r community arts partners. Cantemus’ concerts will take place in Newbury
 port on Saturday\, December 5 at St. Paul’s Church\, 166 High St.\; in B
 everly on Saturday\, December 12 at St. Peter’s Episcopal Church\, 4 Oce
 an St.\; and in Gloucester on Sunday\, December 13 at the Unitarian Univer
 salist Church\, 10 Church St. All three performances are at 4:00 p.m. Cant
 emus’ Artistic Director Jason Iannuzzi said: “Across cultures and cent
 uries\, the year’s longest nights have drawn people together to light ca
 ndles\, gather around tables\, tell stories\, and sing.“ Though customs 
 differ from place to place\, “they often spring from the same desires: f
 or warmth\, for home\, for hope\, and for one another. This concert traces
  this shared story through the eyes of a child.” Allison Iannuzzi said\,
  “There is something truly special about experiencing the wonder of the 
 winter season through a child’s eyes. The Children’s Chorus is delight
 ed to collaborate with Cantemus to share music celebrating the beauty of w
 inter traditions. We hope this music invites us all to rediscover the magi
 c of the season and the shared experiences that unite us.” For more info
 rmation about the program and to purchase tickets\, visit www.cantemus.org
 /tickets. Tickets may also be purchased at the door\, space permitting. Ca
 ntemus gratefully acknowledges sponsorship support from Eastern Bank\, Ins
 titution for Savings\, and Rockland Trust\, and funding support from the L
 ocal Cultural Councils of Newbury\, Newburyport\, Rockport\, and West Newb
 ury. Cantemus participates in the Card to Culture program\, a Mass Cultura
 l Council initiative that offers reduced-price tickets to card holders. Ca
 ntemus is a member of MASSCreative.org and the Greater Boston Choral Conso
 rtium.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/
 event/once-upon-a-winter-tales-of-wonder-through-a-childs-eyes/
LOCATION:St. Paul&#8217\;s Episcopal Church\, Newburyport\, 166 High St.\,
  Newburyport\, MA\, 01950
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/once-upon-a-winter-tales-of-wonder-thr
 ough-a-childs-eyes/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Origami Unfolded Opening Celebration
DTSTART:20261205T103000
DTEND:20261205T160000
DTSTAMP:20261115T180125Z
UID:4c880f3fab26bd21c2b1b4b991bb8f@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Join us as we celebrate the opening of Origami Unfolded! Watch
  origami artists show off their skills during demonstrations\, touch folde
 d pieces at our discovery cart and learn to fold your own paper creations.
  Origami Unfolded investigates recent developments in origami art and orig
 ami-inspired breakthroughs in science\, engineering and math. Origami Herm
 it Crab: Beginner 10:30 am–11:30 am | Create Space Studio Included with 
 admission\; same-day preregistration required. This workshop with artist B
 eth Johnson guides you through creating a detailed origami shell and the c
 lever little hermit crab that calls it home. The geometric shell is surpri
 singly simple to fold\, yet incredibly satisfying\, revealing beautiful hi
 dden structures as you work. Whale on Water: Low–Intermediate 11:45 am
 –12:45 pm | Create Space Studio Included with admission\; same-day prere
 gistration required. This workshop with artist Beth Johnson combines class
 ic origami traditions with a brief introduction to the more abstract techn
 iques of wet folding and curved folding. Participants will fold a cute\, s
 tylized origami whale and an elegant wave base using wet and curved foldin
 g. Hedgehog\, Snail\, and Peacock: Intermediate 3–4 pm | Create Space St
 udio Included with admission\; same-day preregistration required. This wor
 kshop introduces participants to a highly versatile\, corrugated origami p
 attern. From a single geometric foundation\, you will learn how to sculpt 
 up to three animals. Origami Artist Demonstrations 11 am–1 pm\, 2–3 pm
  | Main Atrium Included with admission Watch origami in real time with mas
 ter artists whose work is featured in the exhibition! Lecture and Q&A with
  Bernie Peyton: Why Origami? 1–1:45 pm | Morse Auditorium Included with 
 admission\; same-day preregistration required. The principles of folding h
 ave been fundamental to the design of spacecraft\, fashion\, medical devic
 es\, packaging and architecture. Master origami artist Bernie Peyton explo
 res origami-inspired works and the sparks that led to their creation. Main
  Atrium\, Create Space Studios\, and Morse Auditorium All activities are i
 ncluded with admission. Some workshops require same-day preregistration.\n
 \nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event/or
 igami-unfolded-opening-celebration/
LOCATION:Peabody Essex Museum\, East India Square\, 161 Essex Street\, Sal
 em\, MA\, 01970
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/origami-unfolded-opening-celebration/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Phil Vassar with Annie Brobst
DTSTART:20261205T200000
DTEND:20261205T220000
DTSTAMP:20261115T180125Z
UID:050b1074c0cf3e2986da43e05e6263@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Phil Vassar’s life has always been steeped in music. Raised 
 in a home where his father sang and his two sisters spun everything from M
 otown to West Coast harmonies\, his upbringing was a constant melody. The 
 gospel and bluegrass influence of Lynchburg’s unique geography further s
 haped his sound\, while artists like Bruce Hornsby\, the Statler Brothers
 \, Billy Joel\, Elton John\, and Lionel Richie sculpted his melodic sensib
 ilities. A true fan of rock legends like Queen\, Boston\, and David Bowie
 \, Vassar’s eclectic roots would lay the foundation for his remarkable c
 areer. This December show will include Phil Vassar originals\, along with 
 some Holiday surprises. Annie Brobst New England Music Awards’ Overall A
 ct of the Year\, Female Performer of the Year\, Country Artist of the Year
 \, and 2-time Song of the Year winner\, Brobst’s unique songwriting rang
 es from traditional country to pop country. With influences like Miranda L
 ambert\, Ashley McBryde and Jason Isbell\; she adds a feminine touch to a 
 bro-country sound and a powerful authenticity to storytelling through song
 .\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event
 /phil-vassar-with-annie-brobst/
LOCATION:The Cut\, 177 Main Street\, Gloucester\, MA\, 01930
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/phil-vassar-with-annie-brobst/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:(start)over by Jessica Algard
DTSTART:20261205T193000
DTEND:20261205T210000
DTSTAMP:20261115T180125Z
UID:afc6a953a3691e96e5e1df671811a2@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Young Woman has just moved to a new city where she is going to
  start a new job\, make new friends and begin a NEW phase in her long-term
  relationship. The world is full of possibilities—and that’s really sc
 ary! Young Woman\, with the misguided help of her Gaggle (a trio of figure
 s that appear from her moving boxes)\, tries to start her life over. Can s
 he live up to her boss’s expectations? Will anyone want to be her friend
 ? Does she want to marry Young Man? Will she ever unpack all her boxes?! (
 start)over is a look at life in your 20s\, when everything is constantly c
 hanging\, for the better and worse. This play rushes through time and mixe
 s the mundane with the momentous to recreate the feeling of those tumultuo
 us years and all the ways we try to talk ourselves through them. Performin
 g December 4th to 6th\, all performances are at The Natti-Willsky Performa
 nce Center\, Home of Gloucester Stage Company\, 267 East Main Street\, Glo
 ucester. Showtimes are Friday & Saturday at 7:30pm and Sunday at 2:00pm. T
 ickets can be purchased online or by calling the box office at 978-281-443
 3.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/even
 t/startover-by-jessica-algard/2026-12-05/
LOCATION:Natti-Willsky Performance Center: Home of Gloucester Stage Compan
 y\, 267 East Main St.\, Gloucester\, MA\, 01930
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/startover-by-jessica-algard/2026-12-05
 /
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Yin Yu Tang: A Chinese Home Tour
DTSTART:20261205T111500
DTEND:20261205T161500
DTSTAMP:20261115T180125Z
UID:25843786d0ce5d39d8b6ecb21c4d4f@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Step inside a 200-year-old Chinese home in Salem to experience
  Chinese art\, architecture and culture. For 200 years\, Yin Yu Tang stood
  in the rural village of Huang Cun in a mountainous region of southeastern
  China. Home to eight generations of the Huang family\, this house was dis
 mantled\, transported and re-erected at the Peabody Essex Museum in 2003 t
 hrough a partnership between PEM and the governmental authorities of Huizh
 ou\, in Anhui Province. Yin Yu Tang’s journey constitutes a landmark col
 laboration between Chinese and American architects\, artisans\, conservato
 rs\, engineers and museum experts. The home is presented as it was when th
 e family last lived there in 1982. Plan Your Visit Exercise care Please he
 lp us preserve the house for future generations by refraining from touchin
 g objects and the structure itself. Non-flash photography and videography 
 is permitted for personal use only. Tripods and selfie sticks are not allo
 wed. Please walk with care and watch out for uneven stonework and raised t
 hresholds. Store your belongings\, but keep your coat Yin Yu Tang is parti
 ally outdoors and may be cool in fall and winter. Strollers\, bags\, backp
 acks and umbrellas are not permitted in the house. Please leave them at th
 e coatroom or the cubbies by the Information desk. Our staff at the entran
 ce of the house can look after strollers. Pick up your audio guide in adva
 nce Please pick up your complimentary audio guide\, available in English a
 nd Mandarin\, at the Information desk 10 minutes before your visit. Please
  note: Plan to bring your own pair of headphones. Before your visit\, pres
 s #50 to listen to an introduction to the house. Once inside\, look for st
 one blocks with two-digit numbers. Press the number plus the play > button
  to hear the audio. Be prompt Please be at the doors to Yin Yu Tang five m
 inutes before your scheduled visit with your audio guide in hand. A staff 
 member will alert you shortly before the end of your time slot.\n\nSources
 : North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event/yin-yu-tang
 -a-chinese-home-tour/2026-12-05/
LOCATION:Peabody Essex Museum\, East India Square\, 161 Essex Street\, Sal
 em\, MA\, 01970
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/yin-yu-tang-a-chinese-home-tour/2026-1
 2-05/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Corner’s Grove by Kaela Mei-Shing Garvin
DTSTART:20261206T140000
DTEND:20261206T160000
DTSTAMP:20261115T180125Z
UID:142c08acb4f291fc24270cf28c719f@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:A nod to Our Town\, this new play shifts Thornton Wilder’s m
 asterpiece into the twenty-first century\, to a small town about forty mil
 es south of San Francisco. George\, Emily and their friends are determined
  to live their lives on their own terms and to shed versions of themselves
  they’ve outgrown\, whether that be their high school sweetheart or thei
 r gender identity. Life has only spun forward\, and the only way for the r
 esidents of Corners Grove to keep up is to embrace the unknown and commit 
 to their own truths. Written and directed by Kaela Mei-Shing Garvin .PERFO
 RMANCES November 20 and 21 at 7:30 pm November 22 at 2 pm December 4 and 5
  at 7:30 pm December 6 at 2 pm Friday\, December 4 is SALEM NIGHT. Free ad
 mission for Salem residents. TICKETS $15 general/$10 seniors/free for all 
 students with student ID and under 18.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent 
 page: https://northofboston.org/event/corners-grove-by-kaela-mei-shing-gar
 vin/2026-12-06/
LOCATION:Sophia Gordon Center for Creative and Performing Arts\, 356 Lafay
 ette Street\, Salem\, MA\, 01970
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/corners-grove-by-kaela-mei-shing-garvi
 n/2026-12-06/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Deck the Halls at Hammond Castle Museum
DTSTART:20261206T090000
DTEND:20261206T153000
DTSTAMP:20261115T180125Z
UID:a2bea50374acc827aa7b869adc0603@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Join us for the 2026 Holiday Season as we proudly present our 
 8th annual holiday celebration\, Deck the Halls\, a Museum-wide holiday in
 stallation! Travel back in time to a castle filled with candlelight and lu
 sh\, opulent textures inspired by the great medieval and Renaissance castl
 es of Europe. We are delighted to partner again with Elaine Quinn Interior
 s\, a boutique residential interior design firm based in Manchester-by-the
 -Sea\, to bring heightened inspiration and artistry to this year’s museu
 m-wide holiday installation. Abundant greenery and sumptuous layers of dé
 cor create a richly immersive seasonal experience throughout the Museum. W
 ander through rooms adorned with white birch and Christmas trees\, sparkli
 ng fairy lights\, hydrangea-lace garlands\, and elegant bows\, with each s
 pace transformed by its own unique holiday theme. This celebration also em
 braces the traditions of Hanukkah\, with two beautifully displayed menorah
 s adding their own warm glow to the Museum’s festive splendor. From natu
 re’s bounty of fruits and greenery to the warm glow of candlelight\, eve
 ry detail creates a feast for the senses. Along the way\, encounter a roya
 l guard of nutcrackers\, a gingerbread house replica of the Museum\, and p
 layful elves and gnomes up to mischief\, adding a touch of whimsy for litt
 le ones. In addition to the holiday installation\, guests can enjoy the te
 mporary exhibition\, “Brave-Too-Much”: The Legend of John Coffee Hays.
  Guests should plan for approximately 60 minutes to fully experience the h
 oliday installation and exhibition Extended Tuesday and Thursday Tour Hour
 s: New this year\, visitors can enjoy extended hours on Tuesdays and Thurs
 days\, with last entry at 7:15 p.m. These extended guided and self-guided 
 tour hours offer greater opportunities to experience the Museum after dark
 \, when holiday décor glows with flickering candlelight and twinkling lig
 hts. Upon arrival\, visitors will discover the path and outdoor Courtyard
 \, arches and towers aglow with festive holiday lighting\, creating a trul
 y magical seasonal atmosphere. Visitors will have more time to explore the
  Museum’s holiday decorations and experience the beauty of this historic
  site after dark. Admission pricing varies\, please visit our website for 
 accurate pricing. Guests should plan for approximately 60 minutes to take 
 in the full holiday grandeur. Museum member benefits apply.\n\nSources: No
 rth of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event/deck-the-halls-
 at-hammond-castle-museum/2026-12-06/
LOCATION:Hammond Castle Museum\, 80 Hesperus Avenue\, Gloucester\, MA\, 01
 930
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/deck-the-halls-at-hammond-castle-museu
 m/2026-12-06/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Lúnasa Winter Solstice
DTSTART:20261206T150000
DTEND:20261206T170000
DTSTAMP:20261115T180125Z
UID:916cb7dd2c3d17644e33682cb00f68@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:The beloved Irish folk supergroup Lúnasa\, called the “hott
 est Irish acoustic band on the planet” (The Irish Echo) returns for a fe
 stive holiday concert celebrating the winter solstice. Don’t miss some o
 f the finest pickers\, pipers and fiddlers in the world—Lúnasa will get
  your spirit (and your feet) moving! “Lúnasa reigns as the new Celtic r
 oyalty.” – The Boston Herald Formed in 1997\, Lúnasa has long been on
 e of contemporary Irish music’s leading voices. Named after an ancient C
 eltic harvest festival in honour of the Irish god Lugh\, the group that Fo
 lk Roots magazine once called an “Irish music dream team” was formed f
 rom members of some of Ireland’s greatest bands and over the years has p
 roven its mettle time and again\, having sold over a quarter of a million 
 records and collaborated with notable artists outside the tradition such a
 s Natalie Merchant and Mary-Chapin Carpenter. They have performed more tha
 n 2\,000 shows across 36 countries including high-profile concert venues s
 uch as Carnegie Hall\, The Hollywood Bowl\, National Concert Hall (Dublin)
  and Paris Bercy\, and many of the leading festivals such as Glastonbury\,
  Womadelaide & Edmonton. From the start\, the band’s complex arrangement
 s and unique sound reshaped the boundaries of traditional music and energi
 zed audiences the world over. Critical acclaim followed\, The Irish Echo d
 escribing the band as the “the hottest Irish acoustic band on the planet
 ”\; MOJO magazine named the band “the new gods of Irish music\,” add
 ing that “nobody since the Bothies has wielded such a thrilling rhythmic
  underlay.” Billboard raved that “anybody who listens can’t help but
  find them contagious.” Lunasa’s current lineup features: Trevor Hutch
 inson\, who achieved fame as the bassist with the The Waterboys: piper Cil
 lian Vallely was a member of Riverdance and recorded with Bruce Springstee
 n: Flute player Kevin Crawford\, considered one of Ireland’s greatest fl
 ute players\, was a member of Moving Cloud: award-winning ﬁddler Sean Sm
 yth\, who performed with Donal Lunny’s legendary Coolﬁn and accordioni
 st Alan Kelly\; Colin Farrell is a multiple award winning fiddler\, who ha
 s toured with Project West\, Grada and Michael McGoldrick: Guitarist Ed Bo
 yd made a name for himself in the UK folkscene with bands such as Flook\, 
 Kate Rusby and Cara Dillon.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https
 ://northofboston.org/event/lunasa-winter-solstice-2/
LOCATION:Shalin Liu Performance Center\, 37 Main Street\, Rockport\, MA\, 
 01966
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/lunasa-winter-solstice-2/
END:VEVENT
